specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:47:**Client messages** make sense when the tool result is self-explanatory and the AI does not need to interpret it for the user. A unit conversion, a status update, a formatted data table -- these are cases where the tool knows the best presentation and the AI would just parrot the same information.
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:51:Use plain string returns (the default) when the AI needs to interpret or summarize the tool result for the user. Database lookups and search results are good examples -- the raw data benefits from the AI explaining it in context.
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:74:The key insight: suppressed entries are invisible to the client but visible to the AI. The conversation context stays intact, so the model can reference previous tool results even when the user did not see the AI's commentary on them.
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:78:The `convert_units` tool demonstrates all three features: client messages, suppression, and index assignment. A conversion like "100 miles to kilometers" has a definitive answer -- the AI does not need to elaborate.
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:153:After executing tool calls, `chatOrchestrator.ts` checks each `ToolResponse` for client messages and suppression flags:
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:172:// Signal suppression if any tool requested it
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:183:- `suppress_output` -- sets `state.suppressOutput = true` on the stream state
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:185:When suppression is active, `streamSegment` and `commitAndClear` still call `appendEntry`, `appendDelta`, and `commitEntry` (so the session accumulates entries for context), but skip `sendUpsert`, `sendDelta`, and `sendCommit` (so the client sees nothing).
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:187:When a new entry is created under suppression, the server sends an `entry_skip` message with the server-side index. This tells the client "an entry exists here on the server, but you will never receive it." The client tracks these skipped indices and adjusts all subsequent incoming indices via the `adjustIndex` pure function. Without this, server indices would diverge from the client's entry array, causing deltas and commits to target the wrong positions.
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:189:If the AI streamed text *before* the tool call (which happens when the model explains what it is about to do), that text was already sent to the client as an assistant entry. When suppression activates, the server retroactively blanks these entries by sending `entry_upsert` with empty content. The client already skips rendering empty assistant entries, so the pre-tool text disappears cleanly.
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:191:**Suppression is sticky for the entire stream.** Once a tool sets `suppressAssistantResponse: true` in any round, all subsequent AI responses in that stream are suppressed -- even if later tool rounds do not set the flag. This is intentional: suppression is a per-message property, not per-round. If the first tool already sent the definitive answer to the client, the AI's commentary in all following rounds is equally redundant. A new user message starts a fresh stream with `suppressOutput: false`.
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:193:### stream_end and the spinner
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:195:The server sends a `stream_end` message after every stream, including one that failed after an `error` frame (see [websocket-protocol.md](../hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md)). This is the client's signal to clear loading indicators, so a failed stream clears the spinner the same way a completed one does.
specs/hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md:197:Without `stream_end`, suppression would cause the tool spinner to hang forever. When the assistant response is suppressed, no `entry_upsert` arrives after the tool entry, so the tool stays as the last entry in the array. The client tracks an `isProcessing` state: `true` when the user sends a message, `false` when `stream_end` arrives. The spinner only shows when the tool is both last in the list *and* the stream is still processing.
